WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.224 --> 00:00:01.600
- Good morning Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.901
My one word is believe and I
believe that you have something

00:00:04.901 --> 00:00:06.874
special, that when you unleash it,

00:00:06.874 --> 00:00:08.810
it will be able to change the world.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:10.899
And so to help you on your
journey, today's message

00:00:10.899 --> 00:00:12.482
is break the rules.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.148
Over to you Simon Sinek.

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:16.734
(rooster crowing)

00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:20.000
(upbeat music)
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.642
♫ Entspresso keep me goin'.

00:00:22.642 --> 00:00:26.837
♫ I wake up every morning.

00:00:26.837 --> 00:00:28.573
- Let me tell you a story.

00:00:28.573 --> 00:00:29.926
So a friend of mine and I,

00:00:29.926 --> 00:00:32.762
we went for a run in Central Park.

00:00:32.762 --> 00:00:35.679
The Roadrunners organization,
on the weekends,

00:00:35.679 --> 00:00:38.700
they host races, and it's
very common, at the end

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.102
of the race, they'll have a
sponsor who will give away

00:00:41.102 --> 00:00:44.025
something, apples or bagels or something.

00:00:44.025 --> 00:00:45.878
And on this particular
day, when we got to the end

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:49.109
of the run, there were some free bagels.

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:52.939
And they had picnic tables
set up, and on one side

00:00:52.939 --> 00:00:54.638
was a group of volunteers.

00:00:54.638 --> 00:00:57.485
On the table were boxes of
bagels, and on the other side

00:00:57.485 --> 00:01:01.652
was a long line of runners
waiting to get their free bagel.

00:01:02.787 --> 00:01:05.177
So I said to my friend,
"let's get a bagel".

00:01:05.177 --> 00:01:09.161
And he looked at me and said,
"ah, that line's too long".

00:01:09.161 --> 00:01:11.244
And I said, "free bagel".

00:01:12.458 --> 00:01:16.124
And he said, " I don't
want to wait in line".

00:01:16.124 --> 00:01:18.541
And I was like, "free bagel".

00:01:19.998 --> 00:01:23.158
And he says, "nah, it's too long".

00:01:23.158 --> 00:01:25.335
And that's when I realized
that there's two ways

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:26.936
to see the world.

00:01:26.936 --> 00:01:29.779
Some people see the thing that they want,

00:01:29.779 --> 00:01:32.253
and some people see the
thing that prevents them

00:01:32.253 --> 00:01:34.598
from getting the thing that they want.

00:01:34.598 --> 00:01:36.609
I could only see the bagels.

00:01:36.609 --> 00:01:38.859
He could only see the line.

00:01:40.274 --> 00:01:42.941
And so, I walked up to the line.

00:01:43.842 --> 00:01:47.109
I leaned in between two people,

00:01:47.109 --> 00:01:49.117
put my had in the box,

00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:51.271
and pulled out two bagels.

00:01:51.271 --> 00:01:52.958
And no one got mad at me.

00:01:52.958 --> 00:01:56.427
Because, the rule is, you can
go after whatever you want,

00:01:56.427 --> 00:01:59.876
you just cannot deny anyone
else, to go after whatever

00:01:59.876 --> 00:02:01.197
they want.

00:02:01.197 --> 00:02:02.936
Now I had to sacrifice choice.

00:02:02.936 --> 00:02:04.871
I didn't get to choose which bagel I got.

00:02:04.871 --> 00:02:06.800
I got whatever I pulled
out, but I didn't have

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.792
to wait in line.

00:02:08.792 --> 00:02:11.354
So the point is, you don't
have to wait in line,

00:02:11.354 --> 00:02:13.948
you don't have to do it the
way everybody else has done it.

00:02:13.948 --> 00:02:15.520
You can do it your way.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.050
You can break the rules.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.770
You just can't get in
the way of somebody else

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:20.661
getting what they want.

00:02:20.661 --> 00:02:22.221
That's rule number one.

00:02:22.221 --> 00:02:25.796
- So I'm not necessarily a huge
advocate of cutting in line

00:02:25.796 --> 00:02:28.887
to get what you want, and
I think it's mostly because

00:02:28.887 --> 00:02:32.447
it's getting what you want at
the expense of somebody else.

00:02:32.447 --> 00:02:34.927
Right, like you're cutting in
line, and that means somebody

00:02:34.927 --> 00:02:36.692
else is losing out.

00:02:36.692 --> 00:02:38.598
It's the same thing for me when
I'm driving and there's this

00:02:38.598 --> 00:02:40.526
really long left turn lane.

00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:42.799
I hate when people suggest,
like, hey, just drive up

00:02:42.799 --> 00:02:45.065
on the right side and at the
last second kind of cut in.

00:02:45.065 --> 00:02:48.147
Because, to my own benefit,
I'm screwing over the people

00:02:48.147 --> 00:02:49.460
behind me.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.949
I don't think the world
has to be that way.

00:02:50.949 --> 00:02:54.398
Like, you can go and see
the world as, I'm going

00:02:54.398 --> 00:02:57.540
to make things better for
myself, and in the process,

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.126
help everybody else, instead
of hurt everybody else.

00:03:01.126 --> 00:03:02.584
So I think that's what entrepreneurs do.

00:03:02.584 --> 00:03:04.894
And I think in terms of
breaking the rules, anytime

00:03:04.894 --> 00:03:07.887
there's a rule that I feel
is really holding me back,

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:09.238
I'm frustrated.

00:03:09.238 --> 00:03:11.702
Understand that most rules
were created by people

00:03:11.702 --> 00:03:14.451
who were just like you, who
were no smarter than you,

00:03:14.451 --> 00:03:17.658
and they're created for
the average person, right.

00:03:17.658 --> 00:03:20.938
It's like how average people
should live their life

00:03:20.938 --> 00:03:23.594
and should operate and that
could be very frustrating

00:03:23.594 --> 00:03:26.644
for somebody who doesn't want
to be average at something.

00:03:26.644 --> 00:03:28.136
And so it's understanding,
what are the rules

00:03:28.136 --> 00:03:29.577
that you can break,

00:03:29.577 --> 00:03:31.884
and then, from an
entrepreneur's perspective,

00:03:31.884 --> 00:03:36.067
how do we do that in a way
that can serve other people.

00:03:36.067 --> 00:03:37.621
I think that's one of the
key differences between

00:03:37.621 --> 00:03:41.351
entrepreneurs and the rest of
the world, is that when we see

00:03:41.351 --> 00:03:42.507
something that we don't like.

00:03:42.507 --> 00:03:44.743
When we see a rule that
is really frustrating,

00:03:44.743 --> 00:03:47.207
most people will just complain.

00:03:47.207 --> 00:03:49.522
They'll complain, they'll whine,
they'll tell their friends,

00:03:49.522 --> 00:03:51.208
they'll post on social media.

00:03:51.208 --> 00:03:53.587
They'll just get really
upset, where entrepreneurs,

00:03:53.587 --> 00:03:56.635
we will still get upset,
but we'll use that anger

00:03:56.635 --> 00:03:59.736
and frustration, and turn
it into something positive.

00:03:59.736 --> 00:04:02.038
Out of every crisis, out of
every negativity, out of every

00:04:02.038 --> 00:04:05.880
frustration, there's a
positive, there's something

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.337
that can be created on the
other side that can solve

00:04:08.337 --> 00:04:11.086
that frustration, not
just for us, but for other

00:04:11.086 --> 00:04:12.271
people as well.

00:04:12.271 --> 00:04:15.039
And the bigger that
frustration is, the more people

00:04:15.039 --> 00:04:17.470
complaining about it, the
greater the opportunity there is

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:20.188
to create something really
really meaningful and special

00:04:20.188 --> 00:04:21.510
for the planet.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.477
So I think the key
understanding is to figure out

00:04:23.477 --> 00:04:27.316
which rules you can break,
and which ones you shouldn't.

00:04:27.316 --> 00:04:30.971
If you just follow life around
and you follow every single

00:04:30.971 --> 00:04:34.368
rule, you're never going
to create anything amazing

00:04:34.368 --> 00:04:37.093
extraordinary, insanely special.

00:04:37.093 --> 00:04:39.119
Because you have to be a bit
of a rule-breaker to create

00:04:39.119 --> 00:04:42.264
something new, otherwise you're
just living an average life.

00:04:42.264 --> 00:04:44.984
But if you're breaking every
single rule that ever exists,

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:47.588
you're probably going to end
up dead or in jail, really,

00:04:47.588 --> 00:04:49.066
really, really quickly.

00:04:49.066 --> 00:04:51.167
And so it's understanding what
are the rules you can break,

00:04:51.167 --> 00:04:53.803
and again, having that
perspective of, I'm going to break

00:04:53.803 --> 00:04:57.295
this rule, not just to help
myself, but I'm going to help

00:04:57.295 --> 00:04:59.216
the people around me as well.

00:04:59.216 --> 00:05:01.547
So, the question of the
day today, is I'm curious,

00:05:01.547 --> 00:05:04.933
what is a rule that you have
broken recently, that has

00:05:04.933 --> 00:05:07.115
helped you and helped
the people around you.

00:05:07.115 --> 00:05:08.397
Really curious to find out.

00:05:08.397 --> 00:05:10.008
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:05:10.008 --> 00:05:11.863
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Tom Bennett.

00:05:11.863 --> 00:05:13.734
Tom, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:05:13.734 --> 00:05:14.936
Your One Word.

00:05:14.936 --> 00:05:17.116
I really appreciate the
support, and I hope you enjoy

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:18.011
the read.

00:05:18.011 --> 00:05:19.242
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:05:19.242 --> 00:05:20.295
I believe in you.

00:05:20.295 --> 00:05:22.245
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:05:22.245 --> 00:05:24.069
and whatever Your One Word is,

00:05:24.069 --> 00:05:26.315
much love, and I'll see you guys again

00:05:26.315 --> 00:05:29.789
tomorrow morning for
another shot of Entspresso.

00:05:29.789 --> 00:05:30.622
(upbeat music)

00:05:30.622 --> 00:05:32.391
♫ I wake up every morning.

00:05:32.391 --> 00:05:36.170
♫ Entspresso keep me goin'.

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:39.039
(typing)

00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:41.839
- The people who because the
most successful in our society

00:05:41.839 --> 00:05:43.912
are people who broke the rules.

00:05:43.912 --> 00:05:46.750
You know, there was no presidents
for 24 hour news cycles

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:49.130
until Ted Turner did it.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:51.703
There are so many people
who got out of the box.

00:05:51.703 --> 00:05:54.148
Look at Apple and the birth
of the whole notion of Apple,

00:05:54.148 --> 00:05:56.428
or Nike, I talk about in
the book of the birthing

00:05:56.428 --> 00:05:57.534
of that corporation.

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:59.639
How they did something with
the tennis shoe that had never

00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:00.818
been done before.

00:06:00.818 --> 00:06:04.668
If you only base your
decisions on empirical data,

00:06:04.668 --> 00:06:07.311
you can only repeat what
somebody else already did.

00:06:07.311 --> 00:06:09.550
If you follow your instincts
and your own God-given

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:12.400
creativity, we could go where
we have not been before.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:14.259
And our nation used to do that.

00:06:14.259 --> 00:06:16.400
- We have so many rules
in life about everything.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.901
I say, "break the rules, not
the law, but break the rules".

00:06:19.901 --> 00:06:24.649
My wife has a t-shirt that
says, "well-behaved women

00:06:24.649 --> 00:06:26.951
rarely make history".

00:06:26.951 --> 00:06:30.039
Well you know, I don't want to
burst a bubble, but the same

00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:31.685
is true with man.

00:06:31.685 --> 00:06:34.480
It is impossible to be a
maverick, or true original,

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:36.569
if you're too well-behaved and don't want

00:06:36.569 --> 00:06:38.344
to break the rules.

00:06:38.344 --> 00:06:40.529
You have to think outside the box.

00:06:40.529 --> 00:06:42.097
That's what I believe after all.

00:06:42.097 --> 00:06:44.483
What is the point of being on
this earth, if all you want

00:06:44.483 --> 00:06:47.996
to do is be liked by
everyone and avoid trouble.

00:06:47.996 --> 00:06:51.142
The only way that I ever
got anyplace was to breaking

00:06:51.142 --> 00:06:52.642
some of the rules.

00:06:53.628 --> 00:06:55.863
After all, I remember that
after I was finished with

00:06:55.863 --> 00:06:57.965
my body-building career, I
wanted to get into acting,

00:06:57.965 --> 00:07:00.963
and I wanted to be a star in films.

00:07:00.963 --> 00:07:04.057
You can imagine what the
agents said when I went to meet

00:07:04.057 --> 00:07:05.669
all those agents.

00:07:05.669 --> 00:07:08.828
Everyone had the same line,
that it can't be done.

00:07:08.828 --> 00:07:10.319
The rules are different here.

00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:12.864
He says, "look at your body,
you have this huge, monstrous

00:07:12.864 --> 00:07:15.104
body, you're overly
developed, it doesn't fit

00:07:15.104 --> 00:07:16.801
into the movies".

00:07:16.801 --> 00:07:17.829
You don't understand.

00:07:17.829 --> 00:07:20.736
This was 20 years ago,
the Hercules movies.

00:07:20.736 --> 00:07:23.826
Now, there is the little
guys, to name, Dustin Hoffman,

00:07:23.826 --> 00:07:27.562
Woody Allen, Jack Nicholson,
before he gained weight

00:07:27.562 --> 00:07:29.819
of course, that is.

00:07:29.819 --> 00:07:32.250
But anyway, those are the guys that win.

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:34.142
And the agent also
complained about my accent.

00:07:34.142 --> 00:07:35.454
He says, "no one ever became a star

00:07:35.454 --> 00:07:37.392
with an accent like that, especially not

00:07:37.392 --> 00:07:38.679
with a German accent."

00:07:38.679 --> 00:07:41.728
And yes, I can imagine with
your name Arnold Schwarzen,

00:07:41.728 --> 00:07:44.983
Schnitzle or whatever the
name is, on the billboard.

00:07:44.983 --> 00:07:46.758
Yeah, that's going to draw
a lot of tickets and sell

00:07:46.758 --> 00:07:48.643
a lot of tickets, yeah, right.

00:07:48.643 --> 00:07:51.286
So this is the kind of
negative attitude they had.

00:07:51.286 --> 00:07:53.576
But I didn't listen to those rules.

00:07:53.576 --> 00:07:55.268
Even though there were very
nice, and they said, "look,

00:07:55.268 --> 00:07:57.034
we can get you some bit parts.

00:07:57.034 --> 00:08:00.998
We can get you to be a, playing
a wrestler, or a bouncer.

00:08:00.998 --> 00:08:04.013
Or maybe with your German
accent we can get you to be

00:08:04.013 --> 00:08:08.150
a Nazi officer in Hogan's
Heroes or something like that."

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:10.246
But I didn't listen to all this.

00:08:10.246 --> 00:08:12.844
Those were their rules, not my rules.

00:08:12.844 --> 00:08:14.570
I was convinced I could do it.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.846
That if I worked this hard
as I did in body-building,

00:08:16.846 --> 00:08:18.466
five hours a day.

00:08:18.466 --> 00:08:19.903
And I started getting to work.

00:08:19.903 --> 00:08:21.882
I started taking acting classes.

00:08:21.882 --> 00:08:23.368
I took English classes.

00:08:23.368 --> 00:08:26.908
I took speech classes, dialogue
classes, accent-removal

00:08:26.908 --> 00:08:28.835
classes, I even took.

00:08:28.835 --> 00:08:33.677
I remember running around, "a
fine wine grows on the vine."

00:08:33.677 --> 00:08:35.709
Is it because Germans have
difficulties with the 'F'

00:08:35.709 --> 00:08:37.031
and the 'W' and the 'V'.

00:08:37.031 --> 00:08:39.867
So, "a fine wine grows on the vine".

00:08:39.867 --> 00:08:41.928
I know what some of you
now say, is the hope

00:08:41.928 --> 00:08:43.615
that Arnold got his money back.

00:08:43.615 --> 00:08:45.174
But let me tell you something,

00:08:45.174 --> 00:08:46.981
I had a good time doing
those things and it really

00:08:46.981 --> 00:08:47.814
helped me.

00:08:47.814 --> 00:08:49.749
Finally I broke through.

00:08:49.749 --> 00:08:51.470
I broke through and I started
getting the first parts

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.531
in tv, Streets of San Francisco.

00:08:54.531 --> 00:08:56.330
Lucille Ball hired me.

00:08:56.330 --> 00:08:58.407
I made Pumping Iron, Stay Hungry.

00:08:58.407 --> 00:09:01.286
And then I got the big break
in Conan the Barbarian.

00:09:01.286 --> 00:09:03.805
(audience cheering)

00:09:03.805 --> 00:09:05.729
And there, the Director
said, "if we wouldn't have

00:09:05.729 --> 00:09:08.687
Schwarzenegger, we would
have to build one".

00:09:08.687 --> 00:09:10.007
Now think about that.

00:09:10.007 --> 00:09:13.348
And then, when I did
Terminator, "I'll be back",

00:09:13.348 --> 00:09:16.071
(audience cheering)

00:09:16.071 --> 00:09:19.683
became one of the most famous
lines in movie history.

00:09:19.683 --> 00:09:23.016
All because of my crazy accent.

00:09:23.016 --> 00:09:25.657
Now think about it, the
things that the agent said,

00:09:25.657 --> 00:09:28.784
would be totally a detriment
and would be impossible

00:09:28.784 --> 00:09:32.500
for me to get a job, all of a
sudden became an asset for me.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:34.972
All of those things, my
accent, my body and everything.

00:09:34.972 --> 00:09:37.153
So, it just shows to you,
never listen to that you can't

00:09:37.153 --> 00:09:38.834
do something.

00:09:38.834 --> 00:09:41.096
And,"you have to work your
way up, of course, run for

00:09:41.096 --> 00:09:42.536
something else first".

00:09:42.536 --> 00:09:47.191
I mean, it was the same when
I ran for Govenor, the same

00:09:47.191 --> 00:09:49.587
lines, "you have to work your
way up, it can't be done".

00:09:49.587 --> 00:09:52.216
And then, of course, I ran
for gGovenor, and the rest,

00:09:52.216 --> 00:09:53.297
of course is history.

00:09:53.297 --> 00:09:55.733
They said you have to start
with the small job as Mayor,

00:09:55.733 --> 00:09:58.478
and then as Assemblyman,
and then Lieutenant Govenor,

00:09:58.478 --> 00:09:59.976
and then as Govenor.

00:09:59.976 --> 00:10:03.184
And they said that's the way
it works in a political career.

00:10:03.184 --> 00:10:05.688
I said, "I'm not interested
in a political career. I want

00:10:05.688 --> 00:10:08.939
to be a public servant. I want
to fix California's problems

00:10:08.939 --> 00:10:11.938
and bring people together and
bring the parties together".

00:10:11.938 --> 00:10:13.874
So, like I said, I decided to run.

00:10:13.874 --> 00:10:17.084
I didn't pay attention to
the rules, and I made it,

00:10:17.084 --> 00:10:18.529
and the rest is history.

